  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1049 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCooler weather is finally filtering its way into the region as an\narea of surface high pressure builds across the Midwest, pushing\ncooler air south. This pattern will continue through the next few\ndays with persistent northerly winds keeping the cool air\nthroughout the Mid-South. Temperatures are forecast to range\nbetween 85 F and 95 F along a northeast to southwest gradient\nwith heat indices in the 90s to low 100s. Fortunately, heat index\nvalues across portions of the Mississippi River Delta are not\nforecast to rise above 105 F, eliminating the need for further\nheat headlines beyond tonight. A few stray showers are possible\nThursday evening along the quasi-stationary boundary sitting\nacross the area, but nothing noteworthy is expected from them.\n\nMoving on to Friday, a lee surface wave will develop across the\nCentral Plains and move east into the Middle Mississippi River\nValley through the day. A cold front will eventually develop\nbehind it that is forecast to reach the Mid-South Friday evening,\nswinging through the region overnight into Saturday. Models\ndepict thunderstorms developing along the front as it moves into\nthe region. However, the severe threat does not appear very high\nas mid-level lapse rates remain muted and antecedent cloud cover\nkeeps SBCIN/MLCIN relatively high. Therefore, locally heavy\nrainfall and isolated severe wind gusts are possible given high\nPWATs and large DCAPE, but the coverage of thunderstorms is\nexpected to be low enough to keep a larger severe and flooding\nthreat at bay.\n\nThe front will make its way through the rest of the region\nSaturday. A minimum in convective coverage is likely in the\nmorning hours, picking back up into the afternoon owing to\ndiurnal heating. Once again, the severe threat appears limited\nwithin a regime consisting of weak shear and weak mid-level lapse\nrates, but high CAPE values above 3000 J/kg will still support\nlocally heavy downpours and microbursts as the front clears the\nregion.\n\nBehind the front, cooler temperatures will be reinforced with\nhighs on Sunday expected to remain in the 80s. Afternoon showers\nand thunderstorms are still possible Sunday and into early next\nweek as residual troughing both aloft and at the surface will\nallow for weak diurnal instability (MUCAPE ~1000 J/kg). It isn't\nuntil the middle of next week that the troughing begins to weaken\nand an upper ridge begins to take its place. Temperatures\nincrease in response with highs returning to the 90s after\nmultiple days with highs in the 80s. Humidity levels are expected\nto gradually increase as well, bringing afternoon heat indices\nback into the triple digits by Tuesday and Wednesday to end the\nperiod.",
